+  /// the pixel clock of the screen shown. While `active` and `hdmi` (HDMI
+  /// is shown, 1280 x 720), `x` and `y` name a pixel of the 1650 x 750
+  /// frame in every clock (x of 1280 and above, y of 720 and above:
+  /// blanking), and `color` must be that pixel's colour (red 23:16, green
+  /// 15:8, blue 7:0) exactly `VideoV0.externalLatency` clocks later. While
+  /// `enable` (the core's clock domain) is high and HDMI is shown, `color`
+  /// is shown on the whole screen (the framework's menu overlay on top)
+  /// and the frame buffers are not.
+  ///
+  /// With `ownFrames`, `active` without `hdmi` is the handheld's own
+  /// screen, a rotated panel scanned in columns of the landscape picture:
+  /// `x` is the column (constant through a scan line of the panel, 0 at
+  /// the left edge of the landscape picture; the visible columns are those
+  /// the framework would put a picture of the screen's width in) and `y`
+  /// the row, counting up every clock within the column; the colour is
+  /// due `externalLatency` clocks later as on HDMI. Outside the rows and
+  /// columns shown `x` and `y` have no meaning.
+  /// `sync` rises when a frame of the screen begins, at least four scan
